In the provided code snippet, the comment "@@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array" corresponds to the assert code "Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX);". This is because an IndexOf method in Arrays returns -1 if the element is not found. In this case, the element is presumed to be in the old array if the return value is not negative and the array contains the element. The values are represented by their method calls, New_Ret is the return value from IndexOf, and Old_arrListContainsX is a boolean value.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX);
</code> 
The code in this assert statement checks if the return value from invoking IndexOf method in the ArrayList object is non-negative and x is present in the old array.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListContainsX);
</code>

Note: The assertion might not be true for all inputs due to the randomness of the Pex mutation testing engine. So, it's necessary to run multiple tests to be sure.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && arrList.Contains(x));
</code>
